<div class="news">
  {% if site.news != blank %}
    {% assign news_size = site.news | size %}
    <div
      class="table-responsive"
      {% if include.limit and page.announcements.scrollable and news_size > 3 %}
        style="max-height: 60vw"
      {% endif %}
    >
      <table class="table table-sm table-borderless">
        {% assign news = site.news | reverse %}
        {% if include.limit and page.announcements.limit %}
          {% assign news_limit = page.announcements.limit %}
        {% else %}
          {% assign news_limit = news_size %}
        {% endif %}
        {% for item in news limit: news_limit %}
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" style="width: 20%">{{ item.date | date: '%b %d, %Y' }}</th>
            <td>
              {% if item.inline %}
                {% comment %}
                  Local change: the gem prints `item.content`, i.e. the whole news file. An item with more below an `excerpt_separator` (the certificates in 2026-04-28-graduate-research-baker-scholarship.md) then dumped its full-size images into this table, which pushed the table wider than the page, so every row's text was clipped and the date column squeezed to three lines. Show the excerpt, as the v0 site did; the rest stays on the item's own page. For a one-paragraph item the excerpt is the whole content, so nothing else changes.
                {% endcomment %}
                {{ item.excerpt | default: item.content | remove: '<p>' | remove: '</p>' | emojify }}
              {% else %}
                <a class="news-title" href="{{ item.url | relative_url }}">{{ item.title }}</a>
              {% endif %}
            </td>
          </tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </table>
    </div>
  {% else %}
    <p>No news so far...</p>
  {% endif %}
</div>
